But that makes it too easy. What would be fun, is if some characters
depending on their alignment could bargain with these. For instead,
a player could bargain with a group of roving goblins that if they
leave him alone he will help kill them. Who do you trust now?
An interesting monster that could occur is one that can shape change to
match another playing character. However, the problem is what do you
do with the actual player character? And how do you stop him from
informing the other characters if for some reason he goes out of play?

I also would like to think that seeing how us warriors can have company
then others of this dungeon should also be allowed. For instance,
a shop keeper should be able to have a shop assistant to keep track
of his customers. This will definitely make things abit harder, but noone
wanted it too easy. How about the guard of the vault. He may have an
apprentice, where each takes turns to guard the vaults. Just so people
could have a chance, perhaps both could go off for lunch breaks. I
think that in this "myth" there should be a real bank too. One which
noone could rob, that appears on various levels and which you can 
deposit/withdraw money from. Also, it counts to the final score.
Anyone agree/disagree?

What would be very funny and amusing to add would be addition
characteristics to the succumbus/incumbus (spelling error???).
For instance, when surrounded by say three characters, she
could report to one, after the dirty deeds, that he/she was
excellent and improve his constition, that the next was not good
enough and so lower it slightly, and the third is not his/her
type (too ugly) and have her/his constition lowered.


>	Regarding spells, I think they should effect the whole level. If
>  someone should use a scroll of magic mapping then everyone should see 
>  the fully laid out maze.  If it didn't, what would be the use in banning
>  together? Especially if we couldn't use the abilities of the others?

Well then, if everyone didn't have a share in the effects of the magic,
then it would be up to the player to tell everyone. Once again, it
would be up to do you trust this character or not. For example, after
casting a magic mapping spell, the wizard informs everyone that a shop
is at the left door, and when everyone rushes off in that direction,
he goes off into the right direction (where it truly is) and cleans
it out before everyone gets there.
If everyone is affected by a magic spell casted by one character, then
what happens when he/she casts something like a teleport spell and
everyone is scattered over the dungeon. Does everyone teleport. No,
I don't think so. Perhaps what could be done, is have some spells
where the effects are felt by everyone (within a certain distance
or room) and others that only affect the caster (personally, an
enchant weapon spell, I would only like to use on my weapon
and not everyone else, since weapons do explode after several
enchantments). A side effect of this is that there a various ranges of
spells. For instance, you can get a magic mapping spell, which only you
can see and a magic mapping spell which everyone within a range can see.
A side effect of say, a locate treasure spell which everyone can see,
is that if a leprachaun (spelling???) is in the room, he sees it too
and pops off to clean it up before evryone else does.

Also what happens with other magical goodies?
For example, does a ring of conflict affect everyone apart from
the wearer or does it affect everyone besides the group? If the
first case, do they fight among themselves or what?
What about a ring of invisibility? Is the wearer invisible to
everyone or just those outside the group?

What happens with empowered corpses? If one person eats a dead eye,
does everyone see monsters or just that person. I would imagine it
is only the eater. What happens if two people share in eating a
dead leprachaun, do both start teleporting about or neither.

